  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n6x-6>0\\,\u27f9\\,x>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$",
